          Most do-it-yourself radon tests take 2 to 4 days of exposure, after the house has been closed up for at least 12 hours, and the lab result typically comes back within a week or two of mailing the kit. EPA defines short-term tests as anything from 2 to 90 days and long-term tests as more than 90 days, with the long-term version giving the truest picture of your year-round exposure. Real estate tests run at least 48 hours, and a digital monitor shows a first number within a day or two but needs weeks to produce an average worth acting on. Here is how each option works, and how long you actually wait for a decision.
        </p>

        <div id="test-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Long Does Each Type of Radon Test Take?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Radon tests fall into two groups by duration. Short-term tests stay in the house for anywhere from 2 to 90 days, depending on the device. Long-term tests stay more than 90 days, and often a full year. The device you buy largely sets which group you are in, because the detection method decides how long the test has to run to collect a usable reading and how long it can run before the reading degrades.
          </p>

          <p>
            The common hardware-store kit is an activated charcoal canister or pouch. Charcoal adsorbs radon from the air while the kit is open, so the test is typically 2 to 4 days, sometimes up to 7, as the kit's instructions state. It cannot run much longer because charcoal reaches a balance with the air and starts reflecting only the last few days. Electret ion chamber devices can be used short-term or long-term depending on the chamber, and professional continuous radon monitors record hourly for 48 hours or more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Long-term tests are usually alpha track detectors: a small piece of plastic film that radon decay particles physically mark, which the lab counts afterward. Because the film simply accumulates tracks, it can sit for 90 days to a year, and the longer it sits, the better it averages out seasonal swings. Consumer digital monitors are a third route, measuring continuously for as long as they are plugged in, which covers both short and long-term use from one device.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical version: if you want an answer this week, use a 2 to 4 day charcoal kit. If you want the most reliable answer about what your family breathes over a year, use a 90-day or longer alpha track test, or leave a good digital monitor running through a heating season. Our guide to the <a href="/best-radon-detector/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best radon detectors</a> covers both the mail-in kits and the plug-in monitors.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
