        <div id="before-and-during" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Has to Happen Before and During a Short-Term Test?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Short-term tests only mean something under closed-house conditions. EPA's guidance is to keep windows and outside doors closed, except for normal coming and going, for at least 12 hours before starting a short-term test of 2 to 4 days, and to keep them closed for the whole test. That 12 hours is the hidden part of how long the test takes. An open window brings in outdoor air, which dilutes radon and makes the result read low, so a kit set out on a breezy afternoon with the house open can miss a real problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Run the house normally otherwise. The furnace and central air can run as usual, and a window air conditioner should be set to recirculate rather than bring in outside air. Avoid whole-house fans and anything else that pulls in outdoor air. EPA also advises against short-term tests during severe storms or periods of very high wind, because weather swings the pressure difference between the soil and the house and pushes the reading around. Short tests are also not recommended in very humid or very drafty spots.
          </p>

          <p>
            Placement matters as much as timing. Test in the lowest level of the home you use or could use as living space, which for many houses means a finished or finishable basement. Keep the kit at least 20 inches off the floor, away from drafts, exterior walls, heat sources and high humidity, so not in a kitchen, bathroom or laundry room. A spot in the middle of a basement family room or bedroom is ideal. Then leave it alone for the full exposure period.
          </p>

          <p>
            Write down the exact start time and date on the form when you open the kit, and the end time when you seal it. Labs calculate concentration from the hours of exposure, so a guessed start time introduces error directly into the result. Seal the kit as instructed, usually by replacing the lid and taping it, and get it into the mail the same day. The clock that matters now is the lab's.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Twelve hours closed, then two to four days untouched.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Close windows and outside doors at least 12 hours before a short-term test and keep them closed throughout. Place the kit in the lowest lived-in level, 20 inches or more above the floor, and do not move it until the test is over.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="lab-turnaround" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Long Does It Take to Get Radon Results Back?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a mail-in kit, the result takes roughly as long as the shipping plus the lab's processing, which together usually adds a week or two to the test itself. Most labs email results within a few business days of receiving a kit, and some post them online. So a realistic start-to-answer timeline for a charcoal kit is 12 hours of closed house, 2 to 4 days of testing, 2 to 5 days in the mail, and a few days of analysis: call it one to two weeks from the day you start.
          </p>

          <p>
            The mailing step has a deadline, and it is the most common way people waste a test. Charcoal kits keep losing their captured radon after they are sealed, since radon decays with a half-life of about 3.8 days. Labs correct for that, but only within limits, and most kits state a maximum time between the end of the test and receipt at the lab. Mail it the day you close it, avoid holding it over a weekend if you can, and do not let it sit in a hot car or mailbox.
          </p>

          <p>
            Long-term alpha track tests have no rush on the return trip because the tracks in the film are permanent. Still, the lab needs the start and end dates, and processing can take a little longer than charcoal analysis. Expect the result within a few weeks of mailing. The waiting is mostly in the test itself: three months at minimum before you get any number at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            Professional tests are faster on the back end. A certified tester using a continuous radon monitor downloads the hourly data on pickup and can usually hand over a report the same day or within a day or two. That speed is the main reason real estate testing uses monitors rather than mail-in kits, and it is also why paying a professional can make sense if you need an answer you can act on within the week.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="short-vs-long" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Should You Use a Short-Term or Long-Term Test?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Radon levels are not steady, which is the whole reason test length matters. Concentrations typically run higher at night and in the early morning than in the afternoon. They tend to be higher in winter, when the house is closed up and heated air rising through the house pulls soil gas in at the bottom, the so-called stack effect. Rain, falling barometric pressure and frozen ground can all push levels up for a few days. A 2-day test captures one slice of that; a 1-year test captures all of it.
          </p>

          <p>
            EPA's sequence reflects that. Start with a short-term test, because it is quick and cheap and most homes come back low. If the result is 4 pCi/L or higher, run a follow-up test. A long-term follow-up gives a better picture of your year-round average. A second short-term follow-up gets you an answer faster, and the higher the first reading, the more sense it makes to choose the faster route. Fix the home if the long-term result, or the average of the two short-term results, is 4 pCi/L or higher.
          </p>

          <p>
            A long-term test is the better first test if you are not in a hurry and want certainty. It is particularly worth it in borderline cases, where short-term results land between 2 and 4 pCi/L and one weekend's weather could tip the number either way. It also suits people who plan to stay in the house for years, since the long-term average is the number that actually relates to health risk.
          </p>

          <p>
            Timing within the year helps too. A short-term test in winter, with the house closed and the heat running, tends to read toward the high side of your annual range, which is the conservative direction to err in. A short-term test in a mild spring week with lots of door traffic can read low. If your only result is from a single summer weekend and it came back just under the action level, test again in the heating season before calling the matter closed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="digital-monitors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Long Do Digital Radon Monitors Take to Give a Reading?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Consumer digital radon monitors start working the moment you plug them in, but they do not give a trustworthy number straight away. Most show a first reading within roughly 24 to 48 hours, and many label it as preliminary. Radon is measured by counting radioactive decays inside a small chamber, and at typical household levels a consumer sensor records relatively few events per hour, so early readings carry a wide margin of error. The number settles as the counts accumulate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Treat the readings in stages. The first day or two tells you whether something is wildly high. A one-week average is roughly comparable to a short-term kit result. The meaningful number for decision-making is the long-term average, which most monitors report over 30 days, 90 days or a year. Watching the hourly or daily figure bounce from 1 to 6 pCi/L is normal and not a reason to panic, because the daily cycle and weather drive those swings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where monitors earn their price is in what happens after the first test. They show seasonal patterns, they show how much the level jumps when you finish the basement or change how you run the HVAC, and they confirm that a mitigation fan is still doing its job years later. A one-time kit cannot do any of that. If your home has ever tested above 2 pCi/L, a monitor running permanently in the lowest living level is good value.
          </p>

          <p>
            Their limit is formal use. A consumer monitor is a screening and tracking device, and its results are generally not accepted for real estate transactions or as the official confirmation that a mitigation system worked. For those, use a lab-analyzed kit or a certified professional with a calibrated continuous radon monitor. Note too that an air purifier does not solve radon; the batch guide on <a href="/do-air-purifiers-help-with-dust/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">whether air purifiers help with dust</a> explains what HEPA filters actually remove, and radon gas is not on that list.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="real-estate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Long Does a Radon Test Take When Buying or Selling a Home?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A real estate radon test runs for at least 48 hours. EPA's guidance for home sales allows either a continuous radon monitor running 48 hours or more, or two short-term passive tests, placed side by side at the same time or run one after the other. The house must be under closed-house conditions for 12 hours beforehand and throughout, which in practice means the test fits inside a typical inspection period with a few days to spare.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most buyers use a certified radon measurement professional, usually alongside the home inspection. The tester places a continuous monitor in the lowest livable level, posts notice of the closed-house requirements, and returns after the test to download the data. Professional monitors log hourly readings and often include motion or tamper sensors, so a window opened during the test shows up as a sudden drop in the record. The report usually arrives the same day the monitor is collected or shortly after.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are the seller, expect the buyer's tester to need access twice, once to set up and once to retrieve, and you need to keep the house closed in between. If you test before listing, EPA suggests the results and any mitigation records be made available to buyers. A seller's earlier test can be accepted by a buyer, but many buyers prefer their own, especially if the earlier test is old or the house has been renovated since.
          </p>

          <p>
            When a home-sale test comes back at or above 4 pCi/L, the common outcomes are a negotiated credit, a seller-installed mitigation system, or a buyer installing one after closing. Mitigation itself is usually quick, often installed in a day, and the follow-up test can start 24 hours after the system is running. That is why a high reading rarely kills a sale on its own. It mostly adds a line item and a second test.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="results" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Do the Results Mean, and How Soon Should You Act?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Radon results in the United States are reported in picocuries per liter of air, pCi/L. EPA's action level is 4 pCi/L: at or above it, fix the home. EPA also recommends that people consider fixing homes between 2 and 4 pCi/L, because there is no known safe level of radon and risk rises with exposure. For context, the average outdoor level is about 0.4 pCi/L and the average indoor level in US homes is around 1.3 pCi/L. Radon is the leading cause of lung cancer among nonsmokers, according to EPA.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Result</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What It Means</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Next Step</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Below 2 pCi/L</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low; typical of many homes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Retest after major renovations or every few years</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">2 to 4 pCi/L</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Below the action level, above average</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Consider fixing; a long-term test clarifies</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">4 pCi/L or higher</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">At or above the EPA action level</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Follow-up test, then mitigate if confirmed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Well above 4 pCi/L</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clearly elevated</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fast short-term follow-up, then mitigate promptly</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Radon risk comes from years of exposure, so an elevated result is a reason to act promptly, not to evacuate the house tonight. Run the follow-up test right away, then get quotes from a certified mitigation contractor. The standard fix in homes with basements or slabs is active sub-slab depressurization: a pipe through the slab and a fan that vents soil gas above the roofline. Sealing cracks helps a little but rarely solves it alone, which is why our <a href="/drylok-vs-radonseal/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">DRYLOK vs RadonSeal</a> comparison treats sealers as a moisture product first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Test again after mitigation, and on a schedule after that. The post-mitigation test should start no sooner than 24 hours after the system begins running, and within the first 30 days, to confirm it brought levels down. After that, EPA recommends retesting at least every two years, and again any time you start using a lower level as living space or do significant work on the foundation. A digital monitor makes that ongoing check automatic.
          </p>

          <p>
            One last timing note. Radon levels can differ a lot between neighboring houses on the same street, so a neighbor's result tells you little about yours. The only way to know is to test, and the cheapest kit on the shelf gives you a usable first answer in under a week of exposure. Whatever the number, the test takes far less time than the years of exposure it is there to prevent.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Testing with windows open</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Short-term tests assume closed-house conditions for at least 12 hours before and throughout the test. Open windows, a whole-house fan or a door propped open for a party dilute indoor radon with outside air and make the result read low, which can hide a genuine problem until the next test.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the kit sit before mailing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Charcoal kits lose their captured radon as it decays after the test ends, and labs only correct within a stated window. Holding a sealed kit over a weekend, leaving it in a hot car or forgetting it on the counter can push it past that limit and void the result entirely.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Placing it in the wrong room</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Kitchens, bathrooms and laundry rooms bring humidity and drafts, and floor-level placement reads differently from breathing height. Use the lowest lived-in level, at least 20 inches off the floor, away from exterior walls and vents, and leave the kit undisturbed for the full test period.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating one short test as final</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A single 2-day test captures one slice of levels that swing by time of day, season and weather. A result near 4 pCi/L, or one taken during a mild week with lots of door traffic, deserves a follow-up, ideally a long-term test running through the heating season.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never testing again</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Levels can change after renovations, foundation work, settling or a change in how you use the basement. Mitigation fans also fail eventually. Retest at least every two years, after any major work on the lower level, and within 30 days of installing a mitigation system.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best time of year to test for radon?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Any time works, but the heating season is the most conservative. In winter the house stays closed and warm air rising through it draws more soil gas in, so readings tend to be higher. A short-term test in winter is less likely to understate your risk. A long-term test of 90 days or more covering winter months gives the most representative picture.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I open windows during a radon test?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No, not for a short-term test. Keep windows and outside doors closed for at least 12 hours before and throughout the test, apart from normal entry and exit. Opening them dilutes radon with outdoor air and makes the result read low. For long-term tests of 90 days or more, normal living conditions apply, which is part of why they are more representative.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are home radon test kits accurate?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Lab-analyzed kits from reputable labs are accurate enough for a first screening when you follow the instructions on closed-house conditions, placement, timing and mailing. Most errors come from the user, not the lab. Because radon varies naturally, any short-term result is a snapshot, so confirm results near or above 4 pCi/L with a follow-up test before deciding on mitigation.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a radon test cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A do-it-yourself short-term kit typically costs roughly 15 to 40 dollars, often including lab analysis, though some charge a separate lab fee. Long-term alpha track kits cost a bit more. A professional test with a continuous monitor generally runs from about 100 to a few hundred dollars depending on your area. Some state radon programs offer free or discounted kits.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to test for radon if I have no basement?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Radon enters through any ground contact, including slab-on-grade foundations and crawl spaces, and elevated levels are found in homes of every foundation type. Homes with basements are more likely to test high, but the foundation type does not rule radon out. Test on the lowest level that is used as living space.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long after radon mitigation should I retest?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Start the post-mitigation test no sooner than 24 hours after the system begins running and within the first 30 days. That confirms the fan and piping are pulling levels down. After that, retest at least every two years, or keep a digital monitor running, and test again after any major renovation or change to the foundation.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I live in the house normally during a radon test?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Come and go through doors as usual, cook, shower and run the furnace or central air normally. What you should avoid is opening windows, leaving doors propped open and running whole-house or attic fans that pull in outdoor air. Do not move, cover or handle the test device until the test period is complete.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

